"""Authored expansion of The Last Applause.

Keys address the original, stable source beats. Expanding their prose and spoken
turns preserves core-shot anchors and historical/present cuts. Pagination belongs
to the existing reader; these words never grant evidence or campaign outcomes.
"""

# Close third, present tense. Each addition belongs to the moment immediately
# after its source beat, before the following speech, encounter or site cut.
PROSE = {
 ('EL-01', 'briefing', 0): """The folded paper presses against his ribs when he sits. In another room he would take it out, make something of the interruption, invite a smile before the business began. Here his hands remain on his knees. The mask permits him no useful expression to study in the man across the desk.

The Braun seal has always looked a little provincial to him: too much ornament crowded around a name that ought to have been enough. Yet seeing it beneath the Crow's hand makes his first impulse almost protective. His family has presented a difficulty. Worse, a difficulty that might make Elias appear ordinary. He looks for the signature before he allows himself to look for anything else.""",
 ('EL-01', 'briefing', 5): """Master. He can still hear the word after the room has gone quiet. No audience has ever coaxed that sound from him. Konrad would take it for degradation; Johanna would hear an obligation he was accepting on someone else's behalf. Elias hears the precision of finally addressing a person who cannot be moved into place by his voice.

He wants the Crow to ask why he has brought rehearsal pages. He wants an opportunity to explain how effortlessly he will reconcile the two demands. The file closes. There is no question. He rises at once, irritated with himself for the tiny disappointment and already deciding what a flawless result might persuade the Crow to notice.""",
 ('EL-01', 'briefing', 10): """Johanna's finger remains on the closed case. A shallow indentation marks the place where its handle has rested against her palm. Elias remembers that hand turning pages for him when he was young and impatient with the inconvenience of needing both his own hands at the piano. He has rarely wondered what she had put down to do it.

He reaches for the case. She slides it toward her own chair instead. She has come for his help, and apparently that does not mean surrendering the shape of it to him. The distinction should be obvious. He dislikes how personally it lands.""",
 ('EL-01', 'aftermath', 4): """On the second run he waits for his partner's breath. The impulse to improve the moment is still there: a softer turn of the head, a little generosity for the seats at the back. He leaves it alone. Her answer changes because he has given her room to make it, and the discovery delights him.

That is what the men around his family's table never understood. Acting can surprise him. Their admiration arrives before he does. Here, at least occasionally, somebody can ask for something he has not already decided to give. He looks toward Lotte when the scene ends. She has gone straight to the next cue. He finds this charming until he wants her to look up.""",
 ('EL-02', 'briefing', 0): """He could recover the room with a flick of his wrist. He knows the exact instant when the audience would follow the gesture instead of the woman speaking beside him. There is pleasure in possessing that option, even while declining it. He lets her finish, and when the silence settles, he feels the scene take its weight through both of them.

The young performer at the edge of the rehearsal area has been holding a pencil against her mouth. Elias turns the page toward her so she can see the cue. She smiles before she catches herself. He remembers her name without effort; remembering names makes people feel that the attention was expensive. Often he is pleased to give it. Often he enjoys how grateful they are. The two pleasures have never seemed to him particularly incompatible.""",
 ('EL-02', 'briefing', 4): """What stays with him is the surprise on the teacher's face. He has seen versions of it throughout his life: a wager quietly abandoned, a correction becoming a compliment, an adult recalculating how much could reasonably be asked of him. He learned early to wait for that adjustment. It was the moment the room became his.

Lotte taps the pause in the script again. She is discussing a choice he made this morning. His memory wants to carry her praise back through every room in which he has ever been praised, adding another witness to a verdict already reached. He looks down before she catches him smiling at the wrong thing.""",
 ('EL-02', 'aftermath', 3): """It would be easy to dismiss the company now. People with ordinary gifts, producing ordinary judgements; what could their approval settle? The thought arrives dressed as discernment. He recognises the small meanness beneath it because, a moment earlier, their attention had pleased him.

He stays to repeat the passage. His partner has found a different stress in her line. It changes his answer, and the change is better. He tells her so without making a joke of the concession. Later he will be tempted to describe the discovery as his own. For now he leaves her name beside it in the margin.""",
 ('EL-03', 'briefing', 0): """Her calls have an unglamorous rhythm: a date repeated, a number corrected, a silence while someone at the other end finds a document. Elias can hear the anxious voice rising through the receiver. He would have calmed it in half the time. Then, presumably, Johanna would still have had to find the money.

He studies the room while she works. In a photograph of a family dinner he would have looked like the person at its centre. Johanna would have been half turned toward somebody outside the frame. He has mistaken that position for a preference often enough to stop noticing the mistake.""",
 ('EL-03', 'briefing', 4): """There are people on the schedule whose families he remembers better than their work. One used to bring him tea after long rehearsals; another once repaired a torn cuff while he continued a conversation over her head. He remembers thanking them. He remembers being good at thanking them. The money owed makes those memories suddenly insufficient.

He does not want the chair back. Nothing on the page makes administration seem less tedious. But he begins to understand what Johanna has heard whenever he called her indispensable: that he valued the convenience of never needing to become her.""",
 ('EL-03', 'aftermath', 2): """He had expected the apology to make her smile. It was a graceful little admission, sized precisely so that she could accept it without lingering over the offence. Instead she made him name the vacancy he left.

His first response is to improve her account. He brought distinction to the family. He defended it when its ordinary arrangements failed. All true, and all splendidly irrelevant to the Friday she is trying to reach. He presses the edge of the schedule flat with his thumb. Johanna waits. He has spent years hearing patience as an invitation to speak; today he lets it remain hers.""",
 ('EL-04', 'briefing', 0): """Konrad's handwriting bends extravagantly around the Braun name. Elias can hear the accompanying speech without hearing a recording: the pause before family, the lifted chin on independence, the careful suggestion that everybody listening ought to feel ashamed of needing persuasion.

It is a competent performance. That irritates him more than a bad one would. Konrad has learned where Elias's reputation can hold a room even in his absence. Somewhere in this pledge there will be an appeal to what he owes them, disguised as confidence in what he can do. Elias takes a seat opposite Johanna. The paper between them recalls another demand laid beside another set of pages.""",
 ('EL-04', 'aftermath', 0): """He says it with the authority of a man refusing a role written without his consent. For a satisfying second the distinction feels complete. Then Johanna turns the page and the reserve figures appear beneath it. His signature is absent; the people who believed his name were still persuaded.

He cannot decide that belief was stupid without confronting how carefully he has cultivated it. At family dinners, on steps after performances, in the courtyard after the fighting, he had let them look at him as though his presence settled what would happen next. He had liked that very much.""",
 ('EL-04', 'aftermath', 1): """Elias reads the sentence again. The old victory belongs to him. Konrad's conclusion does not. It should be possible to hold both facts without surrendering either, but the pleasure of the first keeps softening the edge of the second.

Johanna places an empty sheet beside the pledge. She does not ask him to describe how impressive he had been. She asks him to identify what has actually been promised. He begins with the reserve, and for once the most important name on the page is not his own.""",
 ('EL-05', 'briefing', 0): """The service office smells of damp coats and the cardboard sleeves around hot drinks. Beyond its wall somebody rolls an empty case across the loading floor. Each bump through the door joint interrupts the memory he is trying to enjoy.

He has stood in finer rooms receiving less useful information. The carbon records a departure; the duty log records who was there to see it. Neither has been composed for his benefit. He catches himself resenting the absence of an intelligent person who might simply explain the discrepancy and enjoy being understood.

A name on the duty list repeats a name in the pledge. How convenient it would be to treat that as guilt. He could turn a messy family problem into a clean arrangement of loyal people and fools. But the log says who was on shift. It cannot say what they were told. He lays the pages beside one another, carefully enough that their different stains remain visible.""",
 ('EL-05', 'aftermath', 0): """The matched times are small, ugly marks. He had wanted the pattern to reveal itself like the ending of a clever scene: one elegant observation and everyone else suddenly obliged to follow. Instead the work has asked him to compare a thing with another thing and resist improving either.

He thinks of the men who came through the courtyard. He remembers their spacing, their competent adjustments, the moment a leader understood what had gone wrong. He cannot remember all their faces. Johanna can probably name the people who dealt with the damage afterward. That difference in memory says something about the victory he has been telling himself.

He turns the dispatch face down when a trolley passes the office door. The worker pushing it gives him a friendly nod. Elias returns it, warm and effortless. There is no reason to burden this person with the private business of the room. There is also no reason to mistake their smile for an opinion about what he is doing.""",
 ('EL-06', 'briefing', 0): """A circle of bulbs turns the dressing mirror into a flattering proposition. At this distance he can see the line of powder at his collar and one golden strand the brush missed. These are manageable defects. He corrects the strand, then catches the evasion: he has looked at himself because the unsent message gives him no reflection he can control.

He has drafted a simple report three ways. The first sounds eager. The second sounds as if he has worked very hard not to sound eager. The third could have been written by anyone competent. That ought to satisfy him. Instead his thumb hovers over the place where he might make it recognisably his.

Four years have passed since the meeting-room floor. He can perform a stumble convincingly, laugh at an unflattering photograph, invite criticism of a scene. None of these rehearsals in humility reaches the memory he is avoiding. He puts the phone down. In the mirror his shoulder is level. Beneath the coat he remembers exactly where the pressure had been.""",
 ('EL-06', 'aftermath', 0): """The recipient's name has not changed between readings. Nor has the assignment. He leaves the report as it is, then stands very still because sending it has made waiting possible.

Onstage, another actor calls his character's name. That voice expects a reply at a particular moment; it is a demand he understands and, usually, loves. He can offer a whole imagined life in answer. Behind him the phone remains dark. For a brief, humiliating instant he wants to delay his entrance so that he will be present when it lights.

He goes to the door. The movement breaks the spell enough for him to recognise its absurdity. Recognition is a relief, but it is not a cure. He has understood plenty of things he has continued doing. He answers Lotte brightly, checks that his fellow actor has the cue, and takes the private silence with him.""",
 ('EL-07', 'briefing', 0): """The settlement has creases where Johanna folds it. His own memory is smoother: a masked man, a voice, the astonishing fact of a superior. Hers has to fit a filing case and survive being opened when a creditor wants a decision before lunch.

He once imagined that serving the Crow put him beyond the parochial grievances of the house. Sitting opposite Johanna, he hears the self-congratulation in that account. He could leave because she stayed. He could turn defeat into a revelation because she was required to turn it into a working month.""",
 ('EL-07', 'briefing', 3): """She has struck exactly where he would rather she did not. He remembers the angle of a gloved hand more readily than the reporting interval. The interval never defeated him. It has never withheld anything he wanted.

He almost tells her that she cannot understand. Then he considers what the sentence would mean: that his fascination deserved more weight than the loss she was administering. He draws the document closer. The terms do not grow nobler when he reads them in the voice that first spoke them.""",
 ('EL-07', 'aftermath', 2): """There is a moral argument available if he wants one. Continued operations, wages, less disorder, fewer people caught in Konrad's attempt to recover a vanished position. Elias believes these are good reasons to stop the breach. He also knows he would have come if the assignment had offered him much less flattering reasons.

He does not say this to Johanna. It would ask her to admire his honesty at the expense of her security. Instead he checks the obligation she has put beneath his hand. Whatever he calls his loyalty, she must live with its practical consequences.""",
 ('EL-08', 'briefing', 0): """Johanna answers without offering him a compliment for asking properly. That has become a recurring inconvenience. He had been prepared to play the unusually attentive cousin; she seems intent on receiving attention as something due to the work.

The clerk's part in the story is narrow. Elias could widen it with a leading question, a reassuring smile, a suggestion of what a sensible person would already have understood. He has made frightened people grateful for an answer before they noticed whose answer it was. The skill would make this interview easier and its result worse. He hears the Crow's old instruction with uncomfortable clarity: the discrepancy, not the performance of discovering it.""",
 ('EL-08', 'aftermath', 2): """He watches her close the fasteners. Ordinarily he would have taken the case himself and made the gesture look natural. Possession is reassuring. It lets him decide when an object enters the scene, who gets to see it, what will be said before it is opened. Johanna keeps her hand on the handle.

She is trusting him with the account while retaining the original. There is no insult in that arrangement unless he insists that trust must look like surrender. He thanks her. The words sound smaller than his usual thanks and, for that reason, take more effort to offer.""",
 ('EL-09', 'briefing', 0): """He has made the extra work look disciplined. Three neat lines, each underlined once. Any colleague glancing at the sheet might see a conscientious man preparing thoroughly. Lotte turns it over and finds the hour he has already promised to somebody else.

The ugliness of the conflict lies in its scale. No crisis demands his absence. No instruction from the Crow requires these additions. He has imagined a future report in which every possible objection is answered before it forms, and he wants to live in the instant after that report is heard. The company has the misfortune to need him before that instant arrives.""",
 ('EL-09', 'briefing', 4): """He could tell her he carries responsibilities she cannot know. The mystery would help him; it often does. She would either grant the indulgence or be forced into seeming incurious about a burden she had never agreed to carry. He dislikes how quickly the tactic presents itself.

There was a time when admiration from a room like this completed an evening. Now the evening keeps a private blank space. He can fill it with work, with music, with the pleasure of making Lotte laugh, and still find himself checking whether a message has arrived. He does not want less of those things. He wants one person's answer to make them sufficient.""",
 ('EL-09', 'aftermath', 0): """Behind the opaque Hearts mask, his mouth had begun to form a smile before he could stop it. He could remember the title being spoken more sharply than the instructions that followed. A place had been named for him. For one beat that seemed to resolve the question of what he was to the man who had defeated him.

Then the Crow continued describing the work. Reach, influence, people moved without recognising the hand that moved them. Elias listened with a professional appetite of his own. He could do this beautifully. The title had not made them equals, and the fact did not spoil his delight; it gave the delight somewhere to keep looking.""",
 ('EL-09', 'aftermath', 1): """The fold crosses one of his extra preparations. He smooths the crease, irritated by the small damage. Even his attempt to be indispensable has produced something another person must work around.

He is still capable of choosing what to do with an hour. That ought to be a modest freedom. Lately he has treated it as though it belonged to the possibility of being noticed. He puts the call sheet where he will have to see it before he sees the mirror.""",
 ('EL-10', 'briefing', 0): """Elias has prepared a description of the evening that would make the three of them sound like a remarkable ensemble. Mavis is interested in the service latch. Martos has brought the question of what happens after the attractive part is over. Camila leaves a finger beside the figure that Elias had hoped to pass quickly.

He knows all three are capable. His annoyance is not disbelief; it is the experience of finding their attention already occupied by something other than him. He has given them a problem, and they are answering it. A less vain man might find this restful.""",
 ('EL-10', 'briefing', 9): """It costs him a little to remove the flourish. He had pictured the Crow reading it and recognising a particular mind at work. Camila has asked a simple question, and his reluctance to answer has made the private motive embarrassingly visible.

He sets the unnecessary page aside without turning the concession into a joke. There are kinds of usefulness that do not resemble him. The thought is tolerable in principle. Sharing a table with three examples requires more discipline.""",
 ('EL-10', 'briefing', 13): """Sylvanis has spoken with an ease Elias cannot counterfeit in this room. The offence is not merely the words. It is that the Crow has permitted the man who said them to remain exactly as he is.

Elias has spent years perfecting the interval between being addressed and answering. He has examined his own reports for the faintest suggestion of presumption. Now another man stands within the same authority and appears to feel neither gratitude nor shame for taking up space. For an instant Elias wants the Crow to correct him. The desire is petty enough that he hides it even from the hand resting against his sleeve.

He does not know everything that has passed between those two. He supplies a story anyway: indulgence, perhaps, or a freedom that has not yet been tested. Neither story improves the silence. The Crow has no need of Elias to defend his dignity. He gives his report and hears how carefully he is speaking.""",
 ('EL-10', 'aftermath', 2): """His private account would be compelling if he told it well. The gifted boy, the undefeated defender, the single impossible man. He could make a listener feel that understanding him was a rare privilege. He has done that with less honest material.

But a colleague who hears a confession is still a colleague, not a second audience commissioned to supply the first one's missing response. He looks at the names beside the agreed work. He can speak to one of them in private or leave the story untold. Neither choice entitles him to have their part of it arranged around his wound.""",
 ('EL-11', 'briefing', 0): """Konrad stands before sitting, allowing the room time to acknowledge him. Elias knows the technique. He used to watch it from the other end of the family table, delighted whenever it worked on men who considered themselves immune to performances.

The offer is more intelligent than Elias wants to admit. Konrad has brought him a version of himself with the humiliating chapter removed. He can be brilliant, beloved, dangerous and entirely sufficient again. All he must do is pretend the world ended at the courtyard gate.""",
 ('EL-11', 'briefing', 5): """Konrad's expression changes at the word serve. Elias almost enjoys the injury. It is satisfying to disappoint a man who has mistaken your excellence for a family possession. Then the older man's hand closes on the chair back and the satisfaction thins. He had loved Elias when the performance was a ten-year-old's imitation after supper. The love had always contained a claim. It had still been love.

Elias cannot give him the old story back. He does not want to. What the Crow revealed has become the organising fact of his life, and Konrad is asking him to reduce it to an unfortunate incident. For the first time in the conversation he has no wish to sound clever.""",
 ('EL-11', 'aftermath', 0): """Konrad leaves without the small farewell that would have made the argument feel recoverable. Elias remains beside the empty chair. He has refused it twice now, for reasons the man offering it cannot forgive: first because the company was beneath him, then because someone stood above him.

He had called those reasons opposite at first. The room makes them look uncomfortably related. Both begin with where Elias belongs in an order of ability. Somewhere beyond that order Johanna is trying to keep people paid. He straightens the chair, though no one has asked him to, and goes back to the work.""",
 ('EL-12', 'briefing', 0): """He has rehearsed the report while walking to the appointment. Not the facts; those have held steady for hours. The breaths. Where to leave a silence so that accuracy might sound like assurance, where to stop before assurance sounds like pride. In another man's mouth the account would take less work to make plain.

At the desk he resists the urge to improve it once more. The Crow has already begun reading. Elias waits with the unnatural stillness of an actor trying not to be seen waiting.""",
 ('EL-12', 'briefing', 5): """There is comfort in the instruction. He does not need to wonder whether the Crow secretly wanted a more daring outcome. There is also a loss in it: a small private excuse has been taken away. Anything extravagant Elias adds will have his own desire at its centre.

The mask across the desk gives him nothing to negotiate with. He finds himself grateful for that, then suspicious of the gratitude. Even the refusal of reassurance can become another quality to admire if he works at it long enough.""",
 ('EL-12', 'aftermath', 0): """Johanna has underlined the receiving team's name. He would have underlined Konrad's. The difference is practical and also revealing. Elias has been imagining the instant resistance ends; she has been imagining the person who must stand beside it afterward.

He asks her to repeat the timing and listens without polishing the question. This will not make a magnificent story over dinner. It may spare somebody a frightened, dangerous five minutes in a loading court. He can care about that and still want to be admired for caring. The uncomfortable task is to do it when no admiration follows.""",
 ('EL-13', 'briefing', 0): """For once the applause has not happened yet. There is only the heat of the lamps, a familiar line arriving in another person's voice, the slight shift of weight that tells him she will risk the longer silence. He answers the choice she makes. The answer surprises him, though he has spoken its words for weeks.

He loves this. The love is physical: a looseness through the chest, the exact distance between his feet, the sensation of an entire room leaning toward something he cannot accomplish alone. His vanity lives here too. He knows how good he is. But the scene would become smaller if that were all he allowed it to contain.""",
 ('EL-13', 'briefing', 4): """A laugh travels down the corridor, followed by somebody repeating a line from the second act. His line. He smiles before he can decide whether he deserves to. The pleasure is uncomplicated for several seconds.

Then the thought arrives: the Crow has not seen it. Elias lets the smile remain while the pleasure changes underneath. He resents the absent man for occupying the room and immediately wants to retract the resentment. There is nobody here to hear either impulse. Lotte is already speaking about tomorrow. He makes himself listen to her actual voice.""",
 ('EL-13', 'briefing', 8): """Konrad is looking for the boy who would have bowed after being called extraordinary. Elias can feel the old invitation even now. Win beautifully. Make the family proud. Let the witnesses carry the story away in a form that needs nothing from anyone else.

He has no moral innocence to put against it. He has broken people for smaller reasons than the preservation of these operations, and he is serving a man whose ambitions extend well beyond this room. The thought does not weaken his allegiance. It strips one pleasant disguise from it. He will stop Konrad because this is the work he has accepted, because Johanna needs the breach ended, and because he wants the Crow's instruction fulfilled. He cannot reduce those motives to the one that sounds best.""",
 ('EL-13', 'aftermath', 3): """There is no satisfying pose in which to stand while a team finishes its work. He moves aside when asked. Konrad does not look at him as the receiver takes responsibility, and Elias discovers how fiercely he had wanted one last look.

He could have used it: contempt answered with composure, grief answered with a line precise enough to be remembered. Instead a wheel catches at the threshold, someone asks for space, and the moment passes without him. Johanna checks the handover. He waits until she is finished before asking what she needs next.""",
 ('EL-14', 'briefing', 0): """Johanna checks a date twice before signing. Her hand is steady, though the pen leaves a small blot where she pauses. Elias has an absurd impulse to replace the page so that the conclusion can look as orderly as he wants to describe it.

He leaves the blot alone. The work has been done by people in a room, carrying their own anger and fatigue. A clean copy would not make them less present in it. She turns the paper toward him, and he reads before reaching for his pen.""",
 ('EL-14', 'briefing', 4): """He would like to leave her with something generous. Money would come from an account; an embrace would ask her to finish the scene for him. He settles for answering the last question she actually asks.

Outside the office he catches his reflection in the glass. The man looking back is still handsome, still unusually capable, still certain of things for which other people would demand a lifetime's evidence. He has not become modest. He has learned that the people around him continue when he stops looking, and that their continuation can be the part of a story he most needs to attend to.""",
 ('EL-14', 'aftermath', 3): """Lotte puts a pencil behind her ear and starts discussing the next entrance. It would be possible to take offence at how little ceremony she gives his return. He chooses to find it welcoming. The distinction is small and entirely his responsibility.

He offers an imitation of his own worst first rehearsal. She laughs, then corrects the imitation's timing. He laughs too. There are places where being corrected still feels like being invited further in. He is glad this one exists.""",
 ('EL-14', 'aftermath', 8): """He examines the sentence as he once examined a difficult score. Keep it useful. I expect its reach to increase. Continued trust, perhaps. Continued ownership, certainly. He can produce an interpretation that makes the demand feel almost like recognition, and he knows he is producing it.

There is no secret tenderness to discover beneath the mask. The Crow has told him what he intends to possess. Elias has answered. The clarity ought to end the work of interpretation. Instead he finds himself remembering how quickly the next instruction followed the report, as though being expected to return were a distinction all its own.""",
 ('EL-14', 'aftermath', 9): """On the second attempt the line is better. Nobody applauds. He leaves the silence undisturbed for a moment, listening to where the words have landed. He could remain with that pleasure. He does remain with it, long enough to know it is real.

Then he takes out the phone again. The instruction is the same one he has already read. There is nothing new to answer, and he begins composing an answer anyway. At the edge of the light, his reflection offers the expression of a man who has everything in hand. He adjusts it without thinking.""",
}

PROSE.update({
 ('EL-F01', 'text', 0): """The pedals are still an awkward reach from the bench. He dislikes the reminder that the instrument was built for someone larger. The phrase itself presents no such obstacle. He hears where it wants to go before his fingers finish the first movement, and when Anja turns the page, he is already looking past the notes she means to explain.

Her silence pleases him. She does not distribute praise carelessly, which makes obtaining it more interesting. He plays the variation again with his eyes on her hands. When one lifts, he thinks she will stop him. Instead she marks a place in the score. There is still something to do. Good.""",
 ('EL-F01', 'text', 4): """He likes the laugh from the doorway best. It arrives late, as if the person laughing had first needed permission. He repeats the little bow toward her and gets a second, easier laugh. Bringing somebody into the room feels almost as pleasant as holding the room himself.

When the plates begin to move again, he is disappointed. He has another voice ready, a better imitation of a man who visited yesterday. The adults are discussing something he is not meant to follow. He tries the opening anyway. Conversation stops. Konrad laughs first, loudly, and Elias learns something useful about how to interrupt without being called rude.""",
 ('EL-F01', 'text', 6): """In practice he can feel the answer through his feet before the instructor finishes explaining it. Weight moves; a balance can be borrowed and taken away. The adult stops the exercise at the safe point and Elias asks to see it again, impatient with the limit but eager for the correction. He likes learning. He likes how quickly learning turns into something he can demonstrate.

Afterward somebody calls him extraordinary within earshot of a boy still struggling with the first movement. Elias glances toward him. He could offer help. Instead he performs the movement once more, slowly, believing that seeing it done properly ought to be enough. When it is not, he feels a brief irritation he has no reason yet to question.

The house supplies an answer before he asks for one. Elias can do these things because he is Elias. Adults say it affectionately, proudly, sometimes with relief. He begins to hear the affection as confirmation of an order that exists whether anybody explains it or not. Other children wait to discover what they will be good at. He waits to discover what else.""",
 ('EL-F01', 'after_encounter', 0): """He has never needed to invent a past in which he was secretly ordinary. The difficulty is precisely that the adults were right about his gifts. He could hear more, move better, learn faster. What nobody taught him to distinguish was the distance between doing a thing better and being worth more than the person beside him.

Lotte asks for the entrance again. Elias looks at the pencil marks his partner has made. Her preparation is slower than his. It has also found something he missed. He waits while she explains it, resisting the childhood habit of demonstrating that he has understood before she finishes.""",
 ('EL-F02', 'text', 0): """The theatre gives him a room where the work remains unfinished after he enters it. This is an unexpected luxury. At home, people have begun telling him what an occasion will be before he arrives: a triumph, a reassurance, proof that the Braun family has produced something exceptional. He knows how to provide each version. He is growing bored with being right.

Here a missed entrance wastes somebody else's breath. An intelligent objection can come from a person with no reason to flatter his name. Lotte stops him before the turn that would usually earn a laugh. He resents her for a fraction of a second, tries the scene without it, and feels the whole exchange become more alive.""",
 ('EL-F02', 'text', 4): """The chair at the head of the table has been left empty with more care than an occupied chair would require. Konrad gestures toward it while continuing to talk. Elias stays standing long enough to make the invitation explicit.

He knows the men who would sit around him. He has watched them repeat stories of their own nerve, mistaking the fear of employees for proof of a remarkable personality. Some are clever. Some are dangerous. None has ever made him revise his idea of what a person might accomplish. The prospect of spending a lifetime in their company fills him with a contempt so settled that he mistakes it for taste.""",
 ('EL-F02', 'text', 10): """He sees Johanna's expression change as he says it. You understand that work. He means it as praise, which makes her failure to receive it especially irritating. She can make a column of figures settle into an answer; why should she object to being appreciated for it?

Because he has already turned away. The compliment was the door through which he meant to leave. He knows this for an instant and chooses not to remain with the knowledge.""",
 ('EL-F02', 'text', 11): """There are cruelties in the family business he finds vulgar and others he can discuss without discomfort. A clumsy threat offends him more quickly than an effective one. He has never mistaken himself for innocent; innocence would offer very little scope for what he likes about his own mind. What he cannot bear is the assumption that ability obliges him to preside over people whose ambitions seem so small.

The stage offers admiration, certainly. It also offers lives unlike his own, difficult silences, the discovery that a line can become truer when another actor changes it. He wants those things with an appetite the family chair cannot satisfy. He leaves believing he has made an honest choice. He has. He has also arranged for other people to absorb its inconvenience.""",
 ('EL-F02', 'after_encounter', 0): """He does not wish he had become their leader. The memory gives him no appetite for a different life. It merely refuses the flattering shape in which he has kept this one: a gifted man escaping a room too small for him, with everybody left behind understood to be suited to their seats.

Johanna is reading a message. He lets her finish before asking for her attention. It is such a small courtesy that he is embarrassed by the effort of noticing it.""",
 ('EL-F03', 'text', 0): """He has been working on a scene in which a man receives unwelcome news and pretends to have expected it. The family demand strikes him first as an interruption to a more interesting problem. Then he sees Johanna's hand resting flat on the desk, holding herself still while she waits for him to understand.

The routes are familiar. He has travelled them without asking who kept them open. The accounts have paid for a house in which talent could be discovered at leisure. An outside organisation proposes to take the right to decide what happens here. Whatever his opinion of the people who administer it, this is his family's house. The certainty comes easily, without a moral speech to assist it.""",
 ('EL-F03', 'text', 5): """He does not imagine losing. There is a practical question of entrances, spacing, where the guards might obstruct him if they tried to help; he asks those questions carefully. The answer they are intended to secure seems already settled. He has encountered resistance before. It has always become a problem he can solve.

Johanna takes the script with both hands. He is moved by the care she gives it and, almost simultaneously, annoyed that she looks frightened. Surely she has seen enough of him by now. He nearly reassures her with a promise to be magnificent. Instead he asks her to stay clear of the exchange. The request is protective. His impatience with needing to make it is less kind.

At the entrance the first attackers adjust when they see that he has come alone. They are not laughing. He respects that. Their discipline offers a more interesting answer than family gossip suggested. His weight shifts, movement gathers heat, and the comfortable assumption that he knows how this will end settles through his body.""",
 ('EL-F03', 'after_encounter', 0): """For several seconds after the fighting, he listens for the next movement. His body is still solving the space: the loose weapon, the retreating line, the place where a foot would slip if someone returned too quickly. Then the absence of an immediate opponent becomes an audience. Family guards are looking at him from the positions he told them to keep.

He knows how he must look. The knowledge adds pleasure to relief before he has bothered to separate them. Somebody outside the entrance is trying to help a man stand. Elias watches long enough to be sure they are withdrawing, then turns toward Johanna. The scene he will remember is already arranging itself around the moment she sees him unharmed.""",
 ('EL-F03', 'after_encounter', 3): """At the time, being relied upon had felt like an appropriate response to the facts. Now the same reliance is written as a debt. He can remember the gratitude with great clarity. He has more difficulty remembering whether he ever told them where their claim on him ended.

Johanna asks him to read the promise aloud. The words sound more presumptuous in his voice than they did on the page. He dislikes Konrad for writing them. He also dislikes hearing how readily he could have supplied their first half himself.""",
 ('EL-F04', 'text', 0): """There is a change in his pleasure as the courtyard opens before him. The first victories have confirmed his estimate; these remaining forces require him to keep earning it. A pair changes its interval when he moves. Another holds back instead of following the obvious opening. He feels a quick professional interest in whoever arranged them.

He would have preferred them to bring that intelligence to a theatre. Here their competence is attached to the decision to take his family's house, and he can answer it without the inconvenience of wanting them to like him. The freedom is bracing. He moves again, feeding the heat he will need, and their attention tightens around him.""",
 ('EL-F04', 'text', 3): """Konrad has made the coming victory sound like an insult to the door. Elias enjoys the joke, but his eyes stay on the gap. Jokes are safest when one understands exactly what they leave out. The men outside are armed, coordinated and willing to hurt him. His superiority does not make their weapons imaginary.

He tests the ground with the edge of his shoe. If he draws the near pair a step farther, the next one must choose between maintaining its line and closing the space. He likes the clarity of that choice. In conversation people can pretend an answer means something else. Here the body commits and the consequences become visible.""",
 ('EL-F04', 'after_encounter', 0): """The quiet afterward belongs to him in a way applause never quite has. Applause can be polite. This is an entrance they have failed to take, a final formation that has actually broken. He has personally answered every force sent against the house. He allows himself the full weight of that fact.

Johanna counts the deployments aloud. He waits while she finishes, though he knows the number already. He wants her certainty as well as his own. Konrad has begun smiling with the expansive relief of a man who will later tell this story in the first person plural. Elias sees it and does not object. There is enough victory, just now, to be generous with the pronouns.""",
 ('EL-F04', 'after_encounter', 3): """He tests a turn in the empty room, then a second, sharper one. No pain interrupts it. He eats because he is hungry, sleeps because the house is quiet, and wakes expecting another problem he can solve. By the time he dresses for the appointment, fatigue offers him no private qualification to make to the outcome.

He selects a shirt that will leave his shoulders free. The care amuses him: diplomacy dressed for the possibility of discourtesy. He rehearses no threat. Any visitor who has read the reports ought to understand what stands behind the invitation to sit.""",
 ('EL-F04', 'after_encounter', 4): """He has never needed to make the victories smaller to explain what followed. That would be a comfort of a particularly dishonest kind. The forces were serious; he was better. The next man was better than him. He can state the sequence plainly. Living with its final sentence has occupied years.""",
 ('EL-F05', 'text', 0): """He has arranged the chairs so that the visitor will have to cross the clear floor to take the offered seat. It is a small advantage of presentation, the sort he would dismiss as trivial if someone pointed it out. His family's house, his recovered body, his account of the encounter. He expects the room to confirm all three.

The masked man does not hesitate at the doorway. Elias waits for the brief survey that usually tells him how a newcomer intends to impress him. There is no useful offer in the stillness. The visitor has come with an intention already settled, and Elias discovers that he wants to disturb it.""",
 ('EL-F05', 'text', 1): """He looks once toward the edge of the room. No hostage to rescue, no second fighter to blame for an interrupted exchange, no exhausted muscle he has privately decided not to mention. He is almost pleased by the simplicity. If the man wants a demonstration, there will be nothing to misunderstand.

He has thought of the Syndicate as an organisation that made a poor estimate. One more man crossing the same floor should be an extension of that error. Yet the absence of visible haste begins to trouble him. The mask gives him no expression to puncture. He will have to make the body answer.""",
 ('EL-F05', 'text', 4): """He begins with confidence grounded in years of successful movement. The heat is real. So are the training and the judgement with which he commits it. He expects resistance, an adjustment, the increasingly familiar instant when another fighter discovers that recognising Elias's intention is not enough to stop it.

The man offers him no visible invitation to hurry. Elias prepares to change his method if the first is answered; he is far too good to mistake confidence for a reason to stop thinking. An exchange will settle it. He believes this with his whole body, even as the room begins to resist the version of himself he brought into it.""",
 ('EL-F05', 'after_encounter', 0): """The floor is close enough for him to see a pale scratch in the finish. He remembers being irritated by it before the appointment. His mind offers that irrelevant fact with startling clarity while his shoulder refuses the instruction to rise.

He knows what to do with pressure. He has spent years learning where a body gives, how an apparently secure hold opens when the balance changes. He tries. The answer is immediate and complete. His training remains available to him; none of it produces a way out.

For a moment he is furious with the room itself. There should be a pause, a mistake, some unconsidered fact that lets the world return to its proper proportions. Instead the man above him has time to decide what happens next. Elias's breathing becomes shallow. He has never had to understand that time from this position.""",
 ('EL-F05', 'after_encounter', 2): """When he is permitted to stand, he wants to explain the defeat. The explanations present themselves and fail at once. He was rested. The floor was clear. He chose his methods intelligently. Nobody interfered. There is no version in which the result becomes something that happened beside his ability rather than to it.

He ought to hate the man for making this knowledge possible. The thought has a reasonable shape, and he cannot make himself inhabit it. What holds his attention is the economy of the answer: power that does not need to ask whether it has been recognised. Elias has always been extraordinary among the people around him. Here is a person whose judgement might therefore mean something no ordinary admiration could mean.

The idea offers a way to remain exceptional. If someone must stand above him, let it be this man; if Elias alone understands the magnitude of what happened, perhaps that understanding distinguishes him too. He cannot yet hear the vanity rebuilding itself inside the awe. He is busy watching the hand that has released him, afraid of its return and unwilling to look away.""",
 ('EL-F05', 'after_encounter', 3): """He does not miss the pain. What he returns to is the instant in which his own estimate ceased to govern the room. He has sought the sensation in thought so often that humiliation has acquired the familiarity of a treasured scene. That familiarity frightens him when he permits himself to name it.

Beyond the door, Lotte speaks to someone else. The ordinary warmth of her voice helps him lift his hand from the table. He answers the knock as himself: amused, attentive, pleased to be wanted. There is no false face to remove. The man who loves that work and the man still listening to the masked visitor inhabit the same reply.""",
 ('EL-F06', 'text', 0): """The room has been put in order. Elias dislikes that at first. An event of such magnitude should have left something visible beyond his own attention. Johanna has removed a used glass and found a page on which to write terms. Her refusal to preserve the room as a monument feels almost like a disagreement with what happened.

He watches the chair the Crow chooses. The movement is unhurried. Elias's body prepares for a threat that is not being made; his mind is already searching for something in the movement that he might understand well enough to imitate.""",
 ('EL-F06', 'text', 2): """While she considers the obligations, Elias is still considering the voice. Each demand arrives without the little invitation to admiration that accompanies authority in men like Konrad. The Crow expects the world to make room. He has just demonstrated why this part of it will.

Johanna turns a page. The sound breaks Elias's concentration and embarrasses him. She is calculating what can continue. He has been admiring the manner in which continuation is being permitted. Both of them belong to the family in this room; only one is keeping track of what it will cost.""",
 ('EL-F06', 'text', 5): """Konrad sees the empty chair and understands enough of its meaning to stay standing a moment too long. Elias wants to spare him the awkwardness and, at the same time, cannot bear to hear him make the loss sound smaller. He has no patience left for the man's customary scale of grandeur.

Johanna asks for a figure. Konrad gives her a grievance. She repeats the figure. Elias watches them and begins to understand that the aftermath will contain a great deal of work that cannot be defeated in a clear room.""",
 ('EL-F06', 'text', 8): """There should be shame in seeking the company of the man who has taken their independence. Elias searches for it, partly because he knows how the scene would be read by anyone outside his own mind. Shame is present, but it has a rival. He wants another encounter with a judgement that does not arrive already softened by his name.

He could call the desire loyalty to a higher standard. He could call it realism. Neither phrase explains why he has remembered every word of a conversation that gave Johanna so much more to write down. He asks what is required before he can compose an answer for himself.""",
 ('EL-F06', 'text', 11): """Competence under instruction. Elias has been competent all his life. The second half of the demand is unfamiliar. It requires him to let another person's intention remain intact even when he could make the execution more dazzling.

He accepts without a flourish. His voice sounds spare enough to belong to a stranger. He wants to hear it do that again. Outside, the family still has accounts to settle and a defeat to live with. He has found something to move toward inside the very event that has taken their freedom, and he has not yet decided how much of that he can admit to them.""",
 ('EL-F07', 'text', 0): """He has dressed with more care than the assignment requires. The knowledge is mildly embarrassing, so he converts it into professionalism. A man who can turn heads ought to understand when to be unobtrusive. This, too, can be done exceptionally well.

The Crow names a schedule and its discrepancies. Elias waits for the larger purpose to follow. Surely there is a reason this work has been given to him: an invitation to perceive what another operative would miss, to reveal the scale of his usefulness without needing it explained. The silence lengthens. He is constructing a test that has not been set.""",
 ('EL-F07', 'text', 3): """He has never been shy. He can enter an unfriendly gathering and find a person through whom to make the whole room receptive. Here he notices the shape of every answer before he speaks it, cutting away possibilities until scarcely anything remains. He can call this discipline. Sometimes it feels more like holding an instrument so tightly that the sound will not carry.

Master. The word gives the relation a form. It also relieves him of the absurdity of pretending they are colleagues of equal weight. He offers it with fear still alive in his body and something perilously close to pride in being the person who has understood why it is due.""",
 ('EL-F07', 'text', 4): """The contractor compliments his voice before answering the first question. Elias knows the compliment is a bid for leniency, and enjoys it anyway. He can make this man comfortable enough to say almost anything. For a moment he wants a full admission, a disclosure so neat that returning with it would make the original assignment seem small.

Then the signed time and the observed time fail to match. That is what he actually has. He asks again without supplying the desired answer. The man becomes less comfortable. Elias allows the discomfort to remain, even though he could remove it so easily. The interview ends with a discrepancy he can support and a motive he cannot. He returns both limits intact.""",
 ('EL-F07', 'text', 7): """In the corridor he reconstructs the exchange. If he had begun with the observation, perhaps the Crow would have looked up sooner. If he had left out the qualification, perhaps the answer would have been sharper. Each revision is a tiny bargain with a conversation that has already ended.

He has been given no reason to think he failed. That is the difficulty. Failure would supply a task; absence leaves him manufacturing one. He plans the next report before there is a next assignment, then catches himself and laughs quietly. Nobody hears it. Without a listener the laugh does not settle anything.""",
 ('EL-F07', 'after_encounter', 0): """He can recognise the old habit in the speed with which he begins interpreting an answer. Useful becomes valued; valued becomes wanted; wanted becomes a place no one else can occupy. The words do not contain that staircase. He builds it himself every time.

Johanna asks whether he needs the entry repeated. He does. Admitting it is less pleasant than pretending he was thinking ahead. He asks her to begin with the date.""",
 ('EL-F08', 'text', 0): """The correction is unflattering to him. He has left it in because removing it would make the report wrong. A small part of him has also imagined the Crow recognising the restraint required to leave it there. Even accuracy can become a presentation if Elias watches himself performing it closely enough.

The page moves across the desk. He follows the gloved finger to the source line and keeps his own hands still. He has no reliable way to read what is happening beneath the mask, yet his attention behaves as though sufficient concentration might manufacture one.""",
 ('EL-F08', 'text', 3): """He repeats useful in the corridor, silently, with the exact interval that followed it. A room full of admiration can become indistinct by morning. This one word remains stubbornly clear. He tries it in the voice of a critic he respects and finds that the substitution takes nearly all its force away.

It is not merely praise withheld until scarcity makes it valuable. The man who said it has seen the limit Elias once believed did not exist. Approval from that direction seems able to reach a place no other judgement has touched. He wants to offer more of himself to it and is alarmed by how little the wish depends on what might be asked.

He does not imagine mutual affection. He imagines being necessary. The distinction lets him preserve his dignity for another few steps.""",
 ('EL-F08', 'text', 4): """The theatre smells of fabric warmed under lamps. Someone is trying to remember where a prop was left. Elias retrieves it from the place he noticed on his way in and delivers it with the solemnity of a royal messenger. The laugh is immediate and welcome. His ease returns so quickly that the private appointment could belong to somebody else's day.

Lotte does not ask what made him happy. She asks him to take his place. He is grateful for that ordinary demand until the opening line finds him still carrying an expression that belongs to another room.""",
 ('EL-F08', 'text', 8): """He is not incapable of receiving affection. He receives it generously, often returns it, sometimes becomes impatient with the claims it brings. The problem is that he has begun treating one form of judgement as the court of appeal for all the others.

Before leaving, he looks back at the empty stage. He could tell the story of the day with its centre here: a good rehearsal, a laugh, a discovery. Instead the account he keeps turning over begins at the desk. He is still talented enough to choose the shape of almost any scene. He has become remarkably reluctant to choose the shape of this one.""",
 ('EL-F08', 'after_encounter', 0): """The paper offers no flattering interpretation. There is a time, a place and a company expecting him. Whatever private magnitude he gives the other demand, the people waiting will experience his decision in minutes.

He can preserve the commitment, negotiate it honestly, or let his desire take the hour and accept what it costs. Lotte waits for an answer she can use. For once he cannot improve the question by performing it beautifully.""",
})


def apply(chapters, memories):
    for (scene, field, index), text in PROSE.items():
        beat = (memories if scene.startswith('EL-F') else chapters)[scene][field][index]
        beat['narration'] = (beat.get('narration', '') + '\n\n' + text).strip()
    for (scene, field, index), speeches in DIALOGUE.items():
        beat = (memories if scene.startswith('EL-F') else chapters)[scene][field][index]
        beat['speeches'].extend([list(turn) for turn in speeches])


